is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Layer-2 bridging on the CS access switch (switch1 br0) running classic STP. Mechanism: With STP enabled and default timers (hello 2s, forward_delay 15s), any edge-port flap or MAC move on the CS LAN triggers a topology change notification (TCN). Linux bridges react by flushing the forwarding database (FBD) and briefly cycling ports through listening/learning, which interrupts forwarding toward the gateway (router0 eth1, 192.168.2.1). That presents to users as “the whole room freezes” for a few seconds, then resumes.

Localization: switch1’s bridge br0 between eth1 (lan_cs) and eth0 (uplink to router0) is the locus. Evidence on switch1: STP is enabled (stp_state=1; brctl showstp br0 shows both ports forwarding). The kernel log records repeated “topology change detected, propagating” and br0 port state transitions (dmesg excerpt), confirming active TCNs. No errors/drops on router0 eth1, and ICMP to 192.168.2.1 is normally clean when no TCN is occurring, which matches intermittent pauses.

Root cause: STP left enabled on an access bridge with no redundant links and without edge/portfast behavior, so routine end-host link events on lan_cs cause TCNs and FDB flushes, transiently breaking traffic to 192.168.2.1 for the whole room.

Corrective fix: Treat host-facing ports as edge to suppress TCNs, or disable STP entirely on this access bridge (safe here since there is only one uplink and no L2 redundancy). On Linux bridge this can be done by disabling STP on br0 (e.g., ip link set dev br0 type bridge stp_state 0 or brctl stp br0 off). If you keep STP for safety, configure eth1 as an edge/portfast port and enable BPDU guard to prevent accidental loops. This will eliminate the periodic pauses. 
=== END ===